WEBVTT

00:05.050 --> 00:08.500
ATA CAD. Terrain changes, the section decides itself.

00:08.550 --> 00:12.910
Alignment and profile. Draw the alignment, let the profile meet the highway standard.

00:13.583 --> 00:23.117
We draw the road centerline on the Karaçam surface with the Alignment command.

00:23.617 --> 00:33.618
We set the alignment options.

00:33.918 --> 00:42.650
We place the centerline points by clicking on the terrain.

00:42.950 --> 00:50.017
We set the drawing color back to the layer.

00:50.517 --> 01:05.717
With the Profile command we place the terrain section in an empty area.

01:06.150 --> 01:17.483
We zoom into the profile view.

01:17.983 --> 01:33.650
We draw the design line by clicking the design elevations.

01:33.750 --> 01:43.150
The program warns at once: the vertical curve K value is below the highway standard.

01:43.350 --> 01:55.250
We drag the curve grip; K grows and the warning clears.

01:55.350 --> 01:59.283
The design line now meets the standard.

01:59.483 --> 02:02.483
Typical section. Define the road once.

02:04.117 --> 02:09.451
Now we define the road. We open the Typical Section window.

02:10.451 --> 02:16.418
We create a new typical section: Karaçam Road.

02:16.618 --> 02:20.918
We add a lane from the palette.

02:21.218 --> 02:25.418
Then a shoulder and a guardrail.

02:25.518 --> 02:27.035
We mirror the right side to the left.

02:27.085 --> 02:30.068
Shoulder width: one metre.

02:30.118 --> 02:35.151
In the Pavement tab we define a new asphalt package.

02:35.551 --> 03:09.052
We enter the wearing, binder, base and subbase layers with their thicknesses.

03:09.252 --> 03:15.102
We link the package to the section; the layers appear in the preview.

03:15.152 --> 03:25.102
Slope ratios: 1:1 in cut, 1:1.5 in fill.

03:25.152 --> 03:34.952
The test ground shows the same section in cut and in fill.

03:35.052 --> 03:38.152
We click OK; the typical section is saved.

03:38.352 --> 03:41.352
Rules. Write the rule, let the program decide.

03:43.052 --> 03:49.252
To define the rules, we open the Karaçam Road typical section.

03:49.452 --> 03:59.052
From the Slope tab we open an empty rule set. Rules decide which section the program uses at every station.

03:59.252 --> 04:08.784
First rule: if the fill height exceeds four metres…

04:08.984 --> 04:16.634
…use a wall instead of a slope. Wall height at most 7.5 metres.

04:16.684 --> 04:32.484
Second rule: if the fill is under two metres, apply a 1:3 flat safety slope.

04:32.684 --> 04:38.251
The third rule is for the cut side.

04:38.551 --> 04:56.450
In cut, a 0.5 by 0.5 m ditch is added at the toe of the slope.

04:56.650 --> 05:06.516
Now we move the ground with the test slider. As the height changes, the section decides for itself which rule applies.

05:06.716 --> 05:20.448
The preview shows the result: in deep fill, a wall replaces the slope. We wrote the rule once; the program decides the rest.

05:20.648 --> 05:23.888
Corridor and cut. Build the corridor, with a dedicated section for the cut.

05:26.981 --> 05:32.415
We run the Corridor command.

05:32.715 --> 05:46.548
We name the corridor and set the sampling interval to ten metres.

05:46.848 --> 05:52.748
The corridor is built.

05:53.048 --> 06:10.315
In the Corridor Section window we select station 1+120.

06:10.515 --> 06:14.681
Here there is a ten-metre cut; the slopes spread sixteen metres to each side.

06:14.881 --> 06:23.482
We define a separate typical section for the cut: Karaçam Cut.

06:23.782 --> 06:38.182
We add the lane, the shoulder and the cut wall.

06:38.382 --> 06:42.282
We mirror; the shoulder is again one metre.

06:42.482 --> 06:45.415
Pavement preset: rural.

06:45.615 --> 06:52.948
We enter the slope ratios.

06:53.148 --> 07:00.948
On the test ground a wall appears in the deep cut.

07:01.148 --> 07:18.215
In the Regions and Targets table we assign the cut section between 960 and 1320.

07:18.415 --> 07:26.748
At the same station there is now a walled cut; width 13.3 metres.

07:26.948 --> 07:31.468
Superelevation and urban segment. Superelevation by the highway standard, a bike lane in town.

07:31.781 --> 07:38.581
We open the Superelevation command for the alignment.

07:38.781 --> 07:47.948
Highway criteria: design speed 70, maximum superelevation 8 percent.

07:48.148 --> 07:51.715
We calculate by the standard; the curve table and the superelevation diagram appear.

07:51.915 --> 08:02.648
Curve info blocks are placed on the plan.

08:02.848 --> 08:20.348
The superelevation band is shown in the profile view.

08:20.548 --> 08:30.114
In Typical Section we add a ready-made section from the library.

08:30.314 --> 08:40.831
The T2-A package includes a bike lane and a sidewalk.

08:40.881 --> 08:45.482
T2-A is added as a new definition.

08:45.682 --> 09:01.631
We assign the first 200 metres of the corridor to this section as the urban segment.

09:01.681 --> 09:02.664
The corridor is rebuilt.

09:02.926 --> 09:06.166
Warnings. A radius outside the rule, an instant warning.

09:07.448 --> 09:11.848
From the Explorer we open the alignment's geometry table.

09:12.048 --> 09:15.332
We deliberately pull the radius below the minimum: 150 metres.

09:15.382 --> 09:22.248
The program warns: radius 150, minimum 170.

09:22.448 --> 09:35.598
A red warning badge appears above the curve on the plan.

09:35.648 --> 09:42.915
We apply the suggested fix; the radius becomes 170 and the warning clears.

09:43.015 --> 09:45.481
The radius now meets the standard.

09:45.681 --> 09:49.081
3D drive. Every surface with its own material.

09:49.881 --> 09:56.497
We select the corridor and open the Properties panel.

09:56.547 --> 10:03.731
Road marking class: rural.

10:03.781 --> 10:08.664
We send the corridor to the 3D Viewer.

10:08.714 --> 10:17.982
We choose the textured view and the daytime sky.

10:18.182 --> 10:26.532
We start driving; at the road start, the bike lane and the sidewalk.

10:26.582 --> 10:32.182
Superelevation in the curve: the road tilts inward.

10:32.382 --> 10:43.682
We switch the marking class to urban.

10:43.882 --> 10:55.632
The edge line is gone; the dashes are now three by three metres.

10:55.682 --> 11:23.682
In the cut: the wall, the weep holes and the guardrail.

11:23.882 --> 11:26.882
Walls. Detail, automatically.

11:27.915 --> 11:37.848
In Typical Section we open the Karaçam Cut definition.

11:38.048 --> 11:46.947
The detailed preview shows the filter, weep holes, gravel and drain.

11:47.147 --> 11:54.647
From closed forms we go to the wall dimensions.

11:54.847 --> 12:03.514
When the front slope is entered too steep, the program gives a safety warning.

12:03.714 --> 12:06.097
We cancel; the value is not saved.

12:06.147 --> 12:18.964
In Corridor Section, at metre 449, the rule wall appears.

12:19.014 --> 12:25.381
As the station changes, the wall height changes too.

12:25.581 --> 12:28.821
Cross-section sheet. Every station picks its own section.

12:30.348 --> 12:35.914
We run the cross-section sheet command.

12:36.114 --> 12:44.080
Sample lines every twenty metres, thirty metres to the left and right.

12:44.280 --> 12:46.881
We click the corner of the sheet.

12:47.081 --> 12:55.615
78 sections, a four-column sheet is created.

12:55.815 --> 13:21.515
The fill and cut cells show the cut and fill areas; every station has picked its own section.

13:21.715 --> 13:24.715
Quantities. What you draw is your quantities.

13:26.615 --> 13:31.098
We open the Earthwork window.

13:31.148 --> 13:36.014
We select the corridor section as the source and calculate.

13:36.214 --> 13:40.981
Cut, fill and net volume are out.

13:41.181 --> 13:46.415
In the closed-form quantities, guardrail, curb and filter items come from the same model.

13:46.715 --> 13:50.915
From terrain to quantities, on one platform.
